CAT = "cemapLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
Q = [
("Under the Partnership Act 1890, a partner in an ordinary partnership is liable for the firm's debts:",
 "Personally and without limit", ["Only up to their capital", "Only for debts they signed", "Only after the firm closes"],
 "Partners in an ordinary partnership have unlimited personal liability for the firm's debts, so a creditor can pursue any partner's own assets. Only an LLP or a company gives limited liability."),
("A director keeps a company trading when they should have known it could not avoid insolvent liquidation. They may be personally liable for:",
 "Wrongful trading", ["Market abuse", "Tipping off", "Misrepresentation"],
 "Under the Insolvency Act 1986, a director who carries on trading when they knew or ought to have known insolvent liquidation could not be avoided can be ordered to contribute to the company's assets."),
("A limited company can own property and be sued in its own name because it:",
 "Has its own legal personality", ["Is owned by its directors personally", "Has unlimited shareholders", "Is registered with the FCA"],
 "Incorporation creates a legal person separate from its shareholders and directors, so contracts and debts are the company's. FCA registration is unrelated."),
("An ordinary power of attorney stops being valid when the donor:",
 "Loses mental capacity", ["Moves to a care home", "Opens a new bank account", "Reaches state pension age"],
 "An ordinary power of attorney ends on the donor's mental incapacity, which is exactly when help is most needed. A lasting power of attorney is designed to continue."),
("In England and Wales, a lasting power of attorney can be used only after it has been:",
 "Registered with the Public Guardian", ["Witnessed by an FCA-authorised adviser", "Approved by the donor's own bank", "Signed by the donor's employer"],
 "Under the Mental Capacity Act 2005 an LPA must be registered with the Office of the Public Guardian before the attorney can act. It may cover property and finance or health and welfare."),
("Two friends agree a casual favour with no thought of legal consequences. Which element of a contract is most likely missing?",
 "An intention to create legal relations", ["A signature witnessed by a solicitor", "A deposit paid by the buyer", "A document stamped by a court"],
 "A contract needs offer, acceptance, consideration, capacity and an intention to create legal relations. Family and social arrangements are presumed not to have that intention."),
("A seller offers a flat and the buyer replies with a lower price. What happens to the seller's original offer?",
 "The offer ends", ["The offer remains open", "A contract is formed", "The buyer must pay the full price"],
 "A counter-offer rejects the original offer and replaces it, so the first offer cannot later be accepted unless it is made again."),
("A contract made with a 16-year-old for goods that are not necessaries is generally:",
 "Not enforceable against the minor", ["Void for both parties from the start", "Enforceable like any other contract", "Valid only if notarised"],
 "Minors lack full contractual capacity, so most such contracts cannot be enforced against them, although the minor can usually enforce them. Contracts for necessaries are an exception."),
("When an agent acts within their authority, the contract binds:",
 "The principal", ["The agent alone", "Neither party", "Only the third party"],
 "An agent creates a contract between the principal and the third party. The agent usually drops out, unless acting without authority or for an undisclosed principal."),
("Which duty does an agent owe to the principal?",
 "Not to make secret profits", ["To pay the principal's debts", "To guarantee every contract", "To act for both sides at once"],
 "An agent is in a fiduciary position: they must act in good faith, follow instructions, avoid conflicts of interest and account for any benefit received."),
("Two owners hold a home as joint tenants. When one dies, their share:",
 "Passes to the survivor", ["Passes under their will", "Goes to the Crown", "Is split among their children"],
 "Joint tenancy carries the right of survivorship, so the share passes outside the will and the estate. Tenants in common hold separate shares that pass under a will or intestacy."),
("Unmarried partners who want each to leave their share of the home to their own children should own it as:",
 "Tenants in common", ["Joint tenants", "Life tenants", "Leaseholders in common"],
 "Tenants in common each own a distinct share, which they can leave by will. With a joint tenancy, survivorship would give the whole property to the other partner."),
("A joint tenancy of a home in England and Wales can be converted into a tenancy in common by:",
 "Serving a notice of severance", ["Making a new will leaving the share", "Changing the mortgage lender", "Paying off the mortgage"],
 "A written notice of severance to the other owner ends the joint tenancy and its survivorship. A will cannot sever a joint tenancy, because the share passes at the moment of death."),
("With leasehold property, the buyer owns:",
 "The right to occupy for a fixed term", ["The land and the building outright forever", "Only the right to rent it out", "A share of the freeholder's company"],
 "A lease gives the right to occupy for a set number of years under its terms, after which the property reverts to the freeholder. Lenders check that enough of the term remains."),
("In a personal bankruptcy in England and Wales, the bankrupt's assets usually pass to:",
 "A trustee in bankruptcy", ["The bankrupt's spouse", "HM Revenue & Customs", "The Financial Ombudsman"],
 "The Official Receiver or an insolvency practitioner acts as trustee, sells assets and shares the proceeds among creditors. Most bankrupts are discharged after a year."),
("An individual voluntary arrangement (IVA) is:",
 "A binding deal with creditors", ["A court order seizing all assets", "A government loan to clear debts", "A form of company liquidation"],
 "An IVA is a binding agreement, supervised by an insolvency practitioner, to pay creditors over time. It avoids bankruptcy if enough creditors by value agree."),
("A company that cannot pay its debts and is wound up by court order is in:",
 "Compulsory liquidation", ["Members' voluntary liquidation", "An individual voluntary arrangement", "A debt relief order"],
 "A court winds up a company in compulsory liquidation, often on a creditor's petition. A members' voluntary liquidation is for solvent companies."),
("In England and Wales, a valid will must usually be signed by the testator in the presence of:",
 "Two witnesses present together", ["One solicitor acting for them", "A notary and one other witness", "Every beneficiary named in it"],
 "The Wills Act 1837 requires writing, the testator's signature and two witnesses present together who also sign. A beneficiary who witnesses loses their gift."),
("In England and Wales, a person's existing will is generally revoked by:",
 "Marriage or civil partnership", ["Their later divorce or dissolution", "Moving abroad permanently", "Buying a new main home"],
 "Marriage or civil partnership revokes an earlier will unless it was made in expectation of that marriage. Divorce does not revoke the will but treats the former spouse as having died."),
("A person dies intestate in England and Wales, leaving a spouse and no children. The spouse:",
 "Inherits the whole estate", ["Shares it with the parents", "Receives only the house", "Gets nothing without a will"],
 "Since the Inheritance and Trustees' Powers Act 2014 a surviving spouse or civil partner takes everything when there are no children. Parents inherit only if there is no spouse."),
("Under the intestacy rules of England and Wales, a cohabiting partner who was not married to the deceased:",
 "Has no automatic right", ["Inherits as if a spouse", "Receives half the estate", "Inherits the home only"],
 "Intestacy gives cohabitees nothing, however long the relationship. They may claim under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975."),
("The person who creates a trust and puts assets into it is the:",
 "Settlor", ["Trustee", "Beneficiary", "Executor"],
 "The settlor transfers assets into the trust. Trustees hold and manage them, and beneficiaries benefit from them."),
("Under an interest in possession trust, the life tenant is entitled to:",
 "The income from the assets", ["All the capital at once", "Choose all future beneficiaries", "Dismiss the trustees at will"],
 "The life tenant takes the income, or use of the asset, for life. The capital passes to the remainderman when the interest ends."),
("Which type of trust gives the trustees the choice of which beneficiaries receive income or capital?",
 "A discretionary trust", ["A bare trust", "An interest in possession trust", "A charitable bare trust"],
 "Trustees of a discretionary trust decide who benefits, when and by how much, from a class of beneficiaries. In a bare trust the beneficiary is absolutely entitled."),
("Writing a life policy in trust for the policyholder's children usually means the proceeds:",
 "Avoid waiting for probate", ["Must go through the estate", "Go to the policyholder's creditors", "Lose all tax benefits"],
 "The trustees can claim the money directly, so it is paid quickly and normally falls outside the estate for inheritance tax. Without a trust it waits for probate."),
]
